                <note rend="inline" type="source">701.2325/27: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Peru</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Moore</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Lima</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1928-07-28T11:00:00-05:00"><hi rendition="#italic">July 28,
                                1928—11 a.m.</hi></date><lb/>[Received 1:10
                    p.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>82. When your 62, July 21, 1 p.m., was brought to the attention of President
                    Leguia yesterday he said that he saw no reason why Chile should not go ahead
                    with the procedure mentioned therein.</p>
                <p>[Paraphrase.] The President was gratified by this indication of good will on
                    Chile’s part and added hopefully these words: “We will see how far she is
                    willing to go.” [End paraphrase.]</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Moore</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
